WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:20.230
It's my absolute
pleasure to introduce

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:24.260
Dr. Harlan Krumholz,
the Harold Hines, Jr.,

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.630
Professor of
Medicine and Director

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:30.140
of the Yale New Haven Hospital
Center for Outcomes Research

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:35.000
and Yale Center for Research
Computing Faculty Co-Director.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.040
In addition to being
those directors,

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.920
he's also the Director of the
Robert Wood Johnson Clinical

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.850
Scholars Program at the
Yale School of Medicine.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:48.150
His research focus
is on improving

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:51.690
patient outcomes, health
performance, and population

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.000
health.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.160
Welcome, Harlan Krumholz.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.150
I'm sorry because I
know how long he spent

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.890
working on that introduction.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:01.628
You can post it online.

00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:41.470
[AUDIO OUT]

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:45.650
Create the change for
society and science,

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.950
and also to do it so
generously, right?

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:49.760
So everything, you
heard from Brian,

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.757
is about this is white boarded.

00:01:52.757 --> 00:01:54.590
I mean, you can put
your own insignia on it.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:55.298
You can put this.

00:01:55.298 --> 00:01:57.230
I mean, it's not
about what he's doing

00:01:57.230 --> 00:01:59.810
for promoting
himself or his center

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.030
but it's about creating
the capabilities.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:06.050
And then Aaron comes
along, and I just

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:10.160
have never seen so
much really courage

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.230
in someone of that position.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:15.800
Because it's easy when
you've got tenure,

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.160
or it's easy at some points
in your career, where you're

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.725
in a secure position
to be espousing what

00:02:21.725 --> 00:02:22.850
are the right things to do.

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:25.820
It's another thing when you are
in exactly the position that

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.850
makes it difficult to do
the right thing because all

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.800
the forces around
you are telling you,

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.430
you're not going to get credit.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.400
You know, because this
is about your credit.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.150
This is a time of your
life to be selfish.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.649
This is the time of life you've
got to worry about yourself,

00:02:38.649 --> 00:02:41.690
in terms of what you're going to
be credited with because that's

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:42.920
the way the career path goes.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.980
And to have that voice,
I think that's what

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:48.680
makes your voice so
powerful because it shames

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.010
us all, really, in a way.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:51.950
Because it makes
us realize, well,

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:55.550
what have we created
as an ecosystem that

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.640
says to people that they
have to worry that if they do

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.220
the right thing for society,
they do the right thing

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:03.500
in thinking about where
science needs to go,

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:07.310
that they might potentially
jeopardize their own positions.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:09.860
Instead of thinking,
again, generously,

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:13.780
that if we all work
together in common cause--

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.060
that our contributions,
yeah, there

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:17.810
will need to be some
understanding of what

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:20.150
those contributions
are, but we don't

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.910
have to feel that we
have to hold tight

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:26.210
our data and our work and
do it to the detriment

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:28.520
of the progress that needs
to be made as a society.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:31.320
So I just salute
you for all of that

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.110
and I think that it's something.

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:36.140
And then of course my
co-director, [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:39.410
showing all these amazing
things about the universe,

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:42.794
and you know, I want to be an
astrophysicist because I just

00:03:42.794 --> 00:03:44.210
want to be able
to show that stuff

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:45.860
and say, you see this box here?

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:48.740
This box represents
the whole universe.

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:50.990
And this is what it was and
then these clumps you see?

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:54.537
That's what the universe is now.

00:03:54.537 --> 00:03:56.120
In my second life,
I want to come back

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.910
if I had the ability
to be able to do that.

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:05.010
So this talk is a sort
of a basic orientation

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.112
to at least what some of
what my own journey was

00:04:07.112 --> 00:04:08.570
and what I think
is important as we

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:10.610
think about this
issue of data sharing.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:12.110
And I'd like to
enlist your thinking

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:15.980
about how do we actually
achieve what we want.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:18.900
Now, I do want I have
some potential conflicts--

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.040
are we posting these slides?

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.754
No, but we'll post
them somewhere.

00:04:23.754 --> 00:04:25.670
Again, this is a thing
where you go like this.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:29.330
And now for some potential
conflicts of interest.

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:30.812
So these are the grants.

00:04:30.812 --> 00:04:33.020
I'm on the Scientific Advisory
for UnitedHealth Care,

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:35.002
and I founded a
company that's trying

00:04:35.002 --> 00:04:37.210
to enable people to acquire
their health related data

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:37.920
and to move it.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.880
And we'll put this up
somewhere for people to see.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.630
But I want to just get
to some terminology

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:44.588
because people get a
little confused when we're

00:04:44.588 --> 00:04:46.030
talking about these issues.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:47.050
And I'm just very--

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:48.522
this is just very simple basics.

00:04:48.522 --> 00:04:50.480
That's the only way I
can really understand it,

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.852
which is we're often
talking about reporting.

00:04:53.852 --> 00:04:55.310
When we're talking
about reporting,

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:58.490
the question is,
you've done something.

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:00.700
Have you told anybody about it?

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:04.220
And the one situation
I'm most interested in--

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:05.040
I'm a cardiologist.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:06.110
I'm a physician.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:09.530
If somebody's performed
experiments on people,

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:11.897
have they told anyone
what they found?

00:05:11.897 --> 00:05:13.730
So there's this issue
of selective reporting

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:15.380
but there's also the
issue of who paid for it

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:16.430
and was there any return on it?

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:18.179
But then there's the
most important issue,

00:05:18.179 --> 00:05:20.450
which is you put
some people at risk

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:22.250
or involve them and have you--

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:23.860
this is the very basic thing.

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:26.712
Does anyone just
know what you did?

00:05:26.712 --> 00:05:28.670
Then the publication is
sort of the next level.

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:31.150
Is it available
anywhere more broadly?

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:33.410
Has it been subjected
to peer review?

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:35.960
And I think pre-prints are
a form of peer review also.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:38.240
I mean, is it out there?

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:41.000
And then there's this
issue of sharing,

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.880
which is are you willing to let
people take a look at the data

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:45.374
and come to their
own conclusions?

00:05:45.374 --> 00:05:47.540
And then Brian's bringing
up this other issue, which

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:50.090
I think is really important
that I've never even listed

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:52.520
on my slide because it's,
for us, it's always been

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:55.880
so far along, but
it's really are

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:58.040
you willing to share the
workflow, the underlying

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:00.600
stuff that led to the
data that you're sharing?

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:03.320
So for example, in
our field, there

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:06.170
may be that there's raw data
that needs to be adjudicated,

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:09.905
that ends up being one of the
data fields in the final data

00:06:09.905 --> 00:06:15.260
set, but there's work underneath
that that actually might

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:18.860
be somewhat discretionary, might
not be just black and white,

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:20.930
and that often is
beyond the view.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:23.851
Now, sometimes the FDA
or others get that data--

00:06:23.851 --> 00:06:25.600
and I'm going to talk
about some of that--

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:29.060
through litigation, but
that's also another tier here,

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:30.260
which is is that it?

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:32.267
So my own journey
about this began

00:06:32.267 --> 00:06:34.100
when I got involved in
the Vioxx litigation.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:36.140
I have to admit, I was
totally oblivious to what

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:38.630
was going on with
Vioxx, and some lawyers

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:40.740
showed up to talk to me.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:43.010
And I'd never been
involved in any legal cases

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:47.430
before, and at first, it
was like I really am not

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:50.360
that interested in
talking about this issue,

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:53.520
and I was busy enough and
it didn't seem like it

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.770
was going to be worth it.

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:56.760
Let's take this off.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:01.420
But then they
started prevailing--

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:02.694
what they convinced me about--

00:07:02.694 --> 00:07:03.860
this thing doesn't come off.

00:07:06.021 --> 00:07:07.520
Did you ever make
a fool of yourself

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:08.770
when you're in front
of all these people?

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:10.936
All right, this thing is
just going to go like that.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:16.360
So what they-- the reason
they prevailed on me--

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.710
the issue was Vioxx was a
drug that was to treat--

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:21.710
it was a second
generation nonsteroidal,

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:23.510
it's a COX-2 inhibitor.

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:26.289
Celebrex, Vioxx,
these are drugs that

00:07:26.289 --> 00:07:27.830
were meant to be
safer on the stomach

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:32.000
and to have the same benefit
for, for example, joint pain.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:38.490
And the thing was, they
told me that what they had

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:41.760
were all the internal emails
and communications and data that

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:44.520
was available within
Merck that had never

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:46.029
been shared externally.

00:07:46.029 --> 00:07:47.820
And all of a sudden,
I mean, I never really

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:50.490
even thought about it
before, but it kind of

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:51.240
got me interested.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:52.620
And I said, well,
I'll get involved

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:54.286
if you promise me
everything I touch can

00:07:54.286 --> 00:07:56.340
go into the public domain,
because I don't think

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:58.590
that there's a wide appreciation
about the way science

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:00.590
is conducted, and I
think that there would

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:02.160
be something to learn here.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.250
So I decided to get involved
with this for that reason.

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:13.084
And then we started
seeing emails like this.

00:08:13.084 --> 00:08:15.500
I'll read it to you because I
know you can't quite see it,

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:19.250
but what this email says,
"memo regarding changes

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:20.820
to the Lane manuscript."

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:23.330
By the way, this guy's a
faculty member here now,

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:24.740
but he was at UCLA then.

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:29.180
But this is Merck discussing
this guy's manuscript.

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:31.010
And I'm OK with the
first two points.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:34.830
Point three, "we can either
say this is 52% or 52%

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:36.830
and give the numbers that
you have in the table.

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:38.240
It's a matter of emphasis.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:40.280
I'm trying to give this
the best face possible

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:44.754
that's compatible with
the truth of the data."

00:08:44.754 --> 00:08:46.670
Now, let's give them the
benefit of the doubt.

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:48.530
I'm not saying they
were going to lie,

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:50.988
or I'm not saying that they
were going to corrupt the data.

00:08:50.988 --> 00:08:53.150
But this is a very
important exchange

00:08:53.150 --> 00:08:56.731
because it reveals something
that's actually, I believe,

00:08:56.731 --> 00:08:57.230
universal.

00:08:57.230 --> 00:08:59.720
I'm trying to give this
the best face possible

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:01.940
that's compatible
with the truth, quote,

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:03.917
unquote, of the data.

00:09:03.917 --> 00:09:05.750
And I was telling Brian
when we were meeting

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:07.940
before, when I first
started on Vioxx,

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:10.270
I was really starting to
get upset with industry.

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:12.860
I said, well, god, this
is an industry issue.

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:14.930
But the deeper I got
into it, I realized

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:18.590
that this is a cultural
issue within science.

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:22.264
These people were sure they
knew what the truth was and were

00:09:22.264 --> 00:09:23.930
trying to understand
how they could best

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:27.920
present their data to be
compatible with that truth.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:29.960
"One can emphasize
what one wants.

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:32.900
It's not clear, from a clinical
perspective, what any of this

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:33.855
means for erosions."

00:09:33.855 --> 00:09:35.780
These are the ulcers
in the stomach.

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:38.630
"So why not try for somewhat
better appearing message?"

00:09:42.725 --> 00:09:44.850
And from point two, as Ned
and I were in agreement,

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:46.830
I would push back on this one.

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:50.430
"I'm surprised that GI adverse
events in a three-month trial

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:53.550
would not be of interest to
practicing gastroenterologists.

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.510
Why not try to take that out?"

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:58.950
But I'm actually not using
this to impugn Merck or even

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:01.800
this faculty member,
who was taped to say,

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:03.107
I'm glad to say whatever--

00:10:03.107 --> 00:10:04.440
I said whatever Merck would say.

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:06.320
He was caught off
camera saying that.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:09.900
But what I'm saying is,
this is a universal truth

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:11.010
within science.

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:13.180
People have strong feelings
about what they want.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.300
They're trying to be
compatible with the truth

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:17.910
but there are multiple
interpretations.

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:20.010
And what I was
sort of discovering

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:22.830
was, that these guys
were just articulating.

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:26.250
We were able to see emails
that you wouldn't normally see.

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:28.380
And then you would
see emails like this.

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:32.370
They're talking about an
adjudication and a protocol.

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:35.237
So they're getting the records
and they're looking at them.

00:10:35.237 --> 00:10:37.320
In this case, they were
sort of unblinded actually

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:40.447
to the results and they
said, "at this point,

00:10:40.447 --> 00:10:42.530
I think it's too late to
reverse what's been done.

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:46.900
Deborah said that you told
her to count this as an MI.

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:49.020
I don't think the
adjudication committee

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:52.320
would have counted this as
an MI, blah, blah, blah.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:55.170
I would prefer
unknown cause of death

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:58.080
so that we don't
raise concerns."

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:01.990
Now, again, I'm just saying
this is Merck saying.

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:03.960
The committee said
this is a heart attack.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:06.470
The concern was Vioxx was
causing heart attacks.

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:07.962
This is in the Vioxx arm.

00:11:07.962 --> 00:11:10.170
They're saying like, maybe
we should call it unknown.

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:12.910
It's a little bit on the
edge here of what it is.

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:15.795
Assume for a minute
they're right or wrong.

00:11:15.795 --> 00:11:17.170
I'm just showing
you that there's

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:19.800
tremendous amount of
discretion at various points.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:23.670
We're used to seeing papers as
sort of monolithic, pristine,

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:27.120
written in stone,
scientific contributions

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:30.690
and what it's not telling you
is the underlying uncertainty

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:34.290
around almost every
aspect of that paper.

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:36.180
Now, I'm not saying
that in the end

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:38.670
you wouldn't get consensus
around a lot of papers,

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:40.320
but I'm also saying
a lot of papers--

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:41.910
and Brian's shown
this very well--

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:44.034
if given to different
groups, they're going to come

00:11:44.034 --> 00:11:46.830
to different
conclusions, in some ways

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:48.960
predicated on their
own cognitive bias,

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.170
their own beliefs.

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:55.110
And it's not an
evil/not evil situation.

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:58.650
I mean, in this case, the
cognitive bias can be strong

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:00.530
because there's also
strong profit motive,

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:02.490
but it's not just
the profit motive.

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:04.850
You're not going to get
promoted at that time in Merck

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:08.790
for raising questions
about a $10 billion a year

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:13.860
drug, or a $10 billion
over five years drug.

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:16.076
You're not going to
get into trouble--

00:12:16.076 --> 00:12:17.700
you're not going get
promoted for that.

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:19.170
In the same way, I'm not
going to get promoted

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:21.960
in an academic institution if
I start writing papers that

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:25.352
impugn my great theory
for which I'm famous for

00:12:25.352 --> 00:12:27.810
and start saying, you know,
all that stuff I've been saying

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:28.810
for the last five years?

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:31.189
I actually think
it's not true, right?

00:12:31.189 --> 00:12:32.730
And again, it's not
maybe that you're

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:35.180
sitting in your office
saying, holy moly,

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:38.772
I gotta make this up,
but the way your eyes

00:12:38.772 --> 00:12:40.230
are seeing the
data, the way you're

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:42.930
thinking about the framing
that you're putting on it

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:44.730
is because of your
deep set belief,

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:47.530
and now, you think you
know what the truth is,

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:50.410
and so you're interpreting
it in that way.

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:53.050
This was an interesting
figure that I saw,

00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:58.330
which basically was looking at,
among the adjudicated events,

00:12:58.330 --> 00:12:59.260
what percent--

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:01.480
this is now adjudicated
heart attacks.

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:04.330
What percent were confirmed?

00:13:04.330 --> 00:13:08.655
And it turned out that they--

00:13:08.655 --> 00:13:10.030
all things being
equal, you would

00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:12.340
think they'd be confirmed
equally likely in the Vioxx

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:14.020
and the placebo group.

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:16.840
But it turns out that
the heart attacks

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:19.660
are confirmed more often
in the placebo group

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:22.010
than they were-- so what
this means is someone says,

00:13:22.010 --> 00:13:23.200
I think there was
a heart attack.

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:23.860
Goes to the committee.

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:25.450
The committee says,
can you confirm

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:27.040
this was a heart attack?

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:29.080
Well, when you look at
it in the one group,

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:31.320
it's confirmed a lot more
often than the other group.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:33.820
It happens to be the group that
favors the company's product

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:36.370
because if you confirm
heart attacks more

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:40.810
commonly in the placebo group
and you're throwing away

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:43.330
the ones that they're asking
about more in the Vioxx group,

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:45.121
Vioxx then has fewer
heart attacks than you

00:13:45.121 --> 00:13:46.520
might expect otherwise.

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:49.960
Now, again, there are many
ways this can seep in.

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:53.950
Not just that someone at the top
is writing an email memo, hey,

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:56.050
make sure you favor Vioxx.

00:13:56.050 --> 00:13:59.200
There's lots of ways it
gets communicated implicitly

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:02.100
into a bias that occurs.

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:03.600
So what we did was
we wrote a series

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:06.375
of papers based
on our experience

00:14:06.375 --> 00:14:09.970
with this, that sort of
taught us a bunch of stuff.

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:12.360
We learned-- by the way,
we wrote a paper in JAMA

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:14.190
about guest authorship
and ghostwriting.

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:16.249
I mean, we discovered
through these emails

00:14:16.249 --> 00:14:17.790
that there were a
whole lot of papers

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:21.090
out there that actually
academics never wrote at all.

00:14:21.090 --> 00:14:22.440
And we had the contracts.

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:24.124
We saw the money
that was passed.

00:14:24.124 --> 00:14:25.540
Basically, they
were given drafts.

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:28.561
They put their name on it, and
we listed them in this paper

00:14:28.561 --> 00:14:29.810
and that made us very popular.

00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:36.660
But we just thought
this was a case study

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:39.810
that we got to see some
of the underbelly of what

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:42.150
was going on in terms of this.

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:44.160
And we did another
paper in Annals,

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:47.130
where we learned that some of
the clinical trials that they

00:14:47.130 --> 00:14:49.020
do are called seeding trials.

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:50.520
Have you ever heard of these?

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:54.240
So the seeding trial
is that they usually

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:56.760
are close to release,
and they said,

00:14:56.760 --> 00:14:59.339
how are we going to get lots
of doctors using this drug?

00:14:59.339 --> 00:15:00.630
Aready the FDA is on the thing.

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:02.070
It's not a pivotal trial.

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:04.707
It's not one the FDA is
going to use for approval.

00:15:04.707 --> 00:15:06.540
And what they do is,
if you ever see a trial

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:09.302
where it seems like they
have a large number of sites

00:15:09.302 --> 00:15:11.260
and a relatively small
number of people they're

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:13.080
enrolling at each site--

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:16.410
we saw the documents that
this trial won an award

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:17.850
in the marketing
division at Merck

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:21.630
because what their goal was
was to get as many doctors as

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:25.080
possible used to prescribing
the drug just at the time

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:27.300
it was going to be sold.

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:28.560
So they would go--

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:31.412
they can't even talk to the
doctors before it's out.

00:15:31.412 --> 00:15:33.120
They can't begin to
start detailing them.

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:34.350
It's not available yet.

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:37.650
But if I've now got 1,000
practices, 1,000 doctors,

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:40.440
I can actually, at that
time, fly you to a resort

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:43.020
and give you days of
lectures about the drug

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:44.820
because you're now
part of a trial.

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:46.770
So I need to educate
you about what you're

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:49.680
going to be doing the
trial on, and you're

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:52.560
going to be getting your
patients on the drug

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:56.100
because you're in the trial,
and I can actually give you

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:58.530
a lot of support for this.

00:15:58.530 --> 00:16:02.300
And then, you will be listed
in the authorship of some paper

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:04.034
somewhere.

00:16:04.034 --> 00:16:05.700
And like I said, it
got a marking award.

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:06.330
It's a really brilliant thing.

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:07.370
But this is another
thing we learned.

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:09.330
I know this has nothing
do with open science,

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:11.280
but I just have to tell
you, this was amazing.

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:13.770
So the open science
part about this

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:16.350
was understanding the
underlying infrastructure.

00:16:16.350 --> 00:16:17.771
What was the workflow of this?

00:16:17.771 --> 00:16:19.020
How did they pick those sites?

00:16:19.020 --> 00:16:21.030
What they did, though,
they picked people

00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:23.446
that they thought were opinion
leaders and prescribers who

00:16:23.446 --> 00:16:24.760
would get that experience.

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:27.730
We published in BMJ
a paper, and this

00:16:27.730 --> 00:16:31.030
is what got us first thinking
about the data sharing issue.

00:16:31.030 --> 00:16:35.590
So the Brits call learnt.

00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:38.264
What have we learnt from Vioxx.

00:16:38.264 --> 00:16:39.430
We didn't make that mistake.

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:43.999
I just want to make
sure you know that.

00:16:43.999 --> 00:16:45.790
This paper was published
in the New England

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:48.730
Journal of Medicine, "Comparison
of Upper Gastrointestinal

00:16:48.730 --> 00:16:52.010
Toxicity of Rofecoxib--"
which is Vioxx--

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:55.385
"and Naproxen in Patients
with Rheumatoid Arthritis."

00:16:55.385 --> 00:16:57.130
This is a very
prominent study that

00:16:57.130 --> 00:17:00.490
was very important in talking
about the use of Vioxx.

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:07.660
And they had an issue where
myocardial infarctions, heart

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:12.069
attacks, were a little bit
more common in the Vioxx group.

00:17:12.069 --> 00:17:14.650
This was confirming
this suspicion

00:17:14.650 --> 00:17:17.349
that Vioxx was going to be
a problem for the heart.

00:17:17.349 --> 00:17:19.450
The writers write
that the incidence

00:17:19.450 --> 00:17:23.349
of myocardial
infarction was lower

00:17:23.349 --> 00:17:26.650
among patients in the naproxen
group-- that's like Aleve.

00:17:26.650 --> 00:17:29.770
You see their framing, wasn't
higher in the Vioxx group.

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:31.780
It was lower in
the naproxen group.

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:38.480
But the issue was that there
was a borderline significance

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:40.215
about the cardiovascular harm.

00:17:40.215 --> 00:17:42.590
But it turned out, when you
actually looked at the data--

00:17:42.590 --> 00:17:44.870
when, in litigation, all
of the data became free.

00:17:44.870 --> 00:17:46.977
We are able to examine it--

00:17:46.977 --> 00:17:49.310
remember, Vioxx is thought
to be better for the stomach,

00:17:49.310 --> 00:17:50.840
worse for the heart.

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:53.480
At the end of the
study, they stopped

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:57.680
counting heart attacks
before they stopped

00:17:57.680 --> 00:17:59.720
counting stomach events.

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:02.180
So they kept counting
the stomach events,

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:04.850
and they got to it was
safer for the stomach.

00:18:04.850 --> 00:18:07.035
But it was sort of borderline
for the heart attack.

00:18:07.035 --> 00:18:09.410
So there were like three extra
heart attacks in the Vioxx

00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:13.430
group that occurred within the
study period, but they said,

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:15.800
it was going to
take them too long.

00:18:15.800 --> 00:18:17.480
So they stopped
three months earlier

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:18.860
counting the heart attacks.

00:18:18.860 --> 00:18:20.510
You never would
know that looking.

00:18:20.510 --> 00:18:23.435
Actually New England
Journal eventually writes--

00:18:23.435 --> 00:18:25.310
and I don't know why
they didn't retract it--

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:28.660
but they wrote what they
called an expression of concern

00:18:28.660 --> 00:18:31.940
that this had not been revealed
at the time of doing it.

00:18:31.940 --> 00:18:35.270
But again, the only way
you can figure this out--

00:18:35.270 --> 00:18:36.542
and they had a reason for it.

00:18:36.542 --> 00:18:37.250
I can't remember.

00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:39.900
The reason was something like--

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:40.760
I can't remember.

00:18:40.760 --> 00:18:43.070
It was a silly
reason because there

00:18:43.070 --> 00:18:46.100
could be no reason for that.

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:49.710
But anyway, they start doing
it, and later on you could see,

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:51.830
when it was done the
right way, the rofecoxib

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:53.630
was much more harmful.

00:18:53.630 --> 00:18:56.450
Meanwhile, what happens is the
company and a major academic--

00:18:56.450 --> 00:18:58.700
first author's an academic--

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:00.620
produces meta-analysis.

00:19:00.620 --> 00:19:03.110
They combine the data
that's out there.

00:19:03.110 --> 00:19:08.480
But Konstam-- well,
it's up here--

00:19:11.090 --> 00:19:12.470
is the first author.

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:14.900
Everyone else basically
is from Merck,

00:19:14.900 --> 00:19:18.470
and they pulled the
meta-analysis together.

00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:21.740
And we can see from
the emails, they're

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:23.270
the ones really
putting it together.

00:19:23.270 --> 00:19:25.340
Marv does edit it.

00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:27.430
He's a responsible
person, I think.

00:19:27.430 --> 00:19:29.090
This is just what he saw.

00:19:29.090 --> 00:19:32.480
But they are selective in
the trials that they do.

00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:35.550
Not all the trials
were being reported.

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:37.850
So they were able to say,
here are the main trials

00:19:37.850 --> 00:19:40.000
you should be looking at
for the meta-analysis.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:41.000
It just wasn't that way.

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:43.430
And they end up saying,
when you compare

00:19:43.430 --> 00:19:46.560
Vioxx, rofecoxib to
placebo, there's no--

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:49.490
in fact, if anything, there
might be a decreased risk.

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:51.860
But they cherry
picked the studies.

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:54.320
So we actually did
a pooled analysis.

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:56.420
Once we've finished
with the trial,

00:19:56.420 --> 00:20:00.150
we had the judge give us all
the data on all the trials,

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:03.320
and most of these had never
been seen before because it

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:04.990
had never been published.

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:07.232
They just put out
the pivotal stuff.

00:20:07.232 --> 00:20:09.190
And this is what I did
with my friend Joe Ross.

00:20:09.190 --> 00:20:11.780
Joe was a Fellow at this time.

00:20:11.780 --> 00:20:16.070
And we identified 30 randomized
placebo controlled trials

00:20:16.070 --> 00:20:19.680
with 20,000 subjects,
15 from osteoarthritis,

00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:21.420
six form rheumatoid
arthritis, three

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:23.610
is Alzheimer's, six others.

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:25.490
Most of them were pretty short.

00:20:25.490 --> 00:20:30.950
18 were published of the 30
before the Vioxx withdrawal.

00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:35.370
So we start looking at
them and we did this

00:20:35.370 --> 00:20:36.570
as cumulative meta-analysis.

00:20:36.570 --> 00:20:39.180
So we sort of start
adding up what

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:41.880
happens if you bring
in each trial given

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:43.530
when it was finished.

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:46.020
And you can see when
you first start,

00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:47.460
you've only got one trial.

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:50.880
There's a 30% increase in
risk, but it's not significant.

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:51.910
And you start adding up.

00:20:51.910 --> 00:20:53.910
By the time you
get to 2000, you've

00:20:53.910 --> 00:21:00.107
got about a 2x increase
in risk and it's 0.07.

00:21:00.107 --> 00:21:02.190
This is about the time
that vigor trial comes out,

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:05.965
that other one comes out, where
the relative risk was five.

00:21:05.965 --> 00:21:06.840
And you start adding.

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:10.569
By 2001, you've got a
significant result in 1.35.

00:21:10.569 --> 00:21:13.110
I don't know whether a p-value--
what it means at this point,

00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:16.555
but the point is that
there's an indication

00:21:16.555 --> 00:21:18.180
that there's a
possibility that there's

00:21:18.180 --> 00:21:19.860
a risk that's becoming strong.

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:22.680
The drug doesn't get
withdrawn until 2004,

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:25.320
but the public
dialogue at this point

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:27.839
is this is an
absolutely safe drug.

00:21:27.839 --> 00:21:30.130
And their own meta-analysis
is suggesting, if anything,

00:21:30.130 --> 00:21:31.980
it could be protective.

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:34.590
And again, I don't want
to impugn the profession.

00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:37.982
I don't want to impugn my
colleague Marv Konstam.

00:21:37.982 --> 00:21:39.690
I'm telling you that
people start looking

00:21:39.690 --> 00:21:41.550
at it in a certain frame.

00:21:41.550 --> 00:21:44.820
I think the Merck people,
many of them good people,

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:48.030
are thinking about no
way this is a problem,

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:50.104
or somehow there's a
cognitive dissonance.

00:21:50.104 --> 00:21:52.020
I've got to be very
careful around Brian using

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:55.170
any of these psychology
words, but he's kind enough

00:21:55.170 --> 00:21:55.980
not to correct me.

00:21:55.980 --> 00:22:00.610
But there's something going
on here that's leading to--

00:22:00.610 --> 00:22:03.460
by the time you got to when
the drug was withdrawn, I mean,

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:06.190
the signal's just
getting stronger anyway

00:22:06.190 --> 00:22:09.040
as more cases are accumulating.

00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:12.880
But this is what kind of
got our group motivated

00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:14.710
in thinking that
we've got a problem

00:22:14.710 --> 00:22:16.510
in the scientific enterprise.

00:22:16.510 --> 00:22:21.250
This lack of transparency,
the unwillingness

00:22:21.250 --> 00:22:26.080
to admit that whoever's doing
the study might not be--

00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:29.770
have the sort of the corner
on the interpretation

00:22:29.770 --> 00:22:30.400
of that study.

00:22:30.400 --> 00:22:32.290
That there may be
more than one way

00:22:32.290 --> 00:22:36.070
to look at things and more
than one way to interpret them.

00:22:36.070 --> 00:22:38.680
Got us thinking that we've
got a big problem here,

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:40.885
and this affects people's lives.

00:22:40.885 --> 00:22:42.260
I'm just going to
say, there were

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:46.070
other studies like this where
this was an RCT of Celecoxib--

00:22:46.070 --> 00:22:47.660
I'm sort of into the
coxibs right now.

00:22:47.660 --> 00:22:50.750
But that led to
this study that was

00:22:50.750 --> 00:22:55.010
in JAMA that
concluded that it was

00:22:55.010 --> 00:22:57.530
much lower incidence of ulcers.

00:22:57.530 --> 00:22:59.780
Main outcome incidence
of prospectively

00:22:59.780 --> 00:23:03.380
defined symptomatic upper GI
ulcers and ulcer complications

00:23:03.380 --> 00:23:05.304
and other things.

00:23:05.304 --> 00:23:06.470
This was their main outcome.

00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:10.070
We're better with Celebrex.

00:23:10.070 --> 00:23:13.010
So this is what they
showed in the JAMA paper.

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:14.660
Look how much
better Celebrex is.

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:15.950
That's amazing.

00:23:15.950 --> 00:23:21.290
But it turned out, they actually
had the 12-month data, too,

00:23:21.290 --> 00:23:25.570
and the 12-month data, it
looks a lot less impressive.

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:28.490
But they just had
decided, why show a year?

00:23:28.490 --> 00:23:29.380
We've got six months.

00:23:32.900 --> 00:23:36.140
So but again, this
is about this stuff

00:23:36.140 --> 00:23:38.845
can't happen if we're
starting to share data.

00:23:38.845 --> 00:23:39.470
It's out there.

00:23:39.470 --> 00:23:41.210
I mean, there's
nothing in it that's

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:42.980
for anyone who's
going to try to do it.

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:44.630
And if someone did
feel like six months

00:23:44.630 --> 00:23:46.760
was somehow telling you
something important,

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:48.229
let them make the case.

00:23:48.229 --> 00:23:50.270
But these are the kind of
things that's come out.

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:52.940
You may have seen this one
more recently with Paxil.

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:56.270
This was a study that was
published more than a decade

00:23:56.270 --> 00:23:58.550
ago that was
suggesting that Paxil

00:23:58.550 --> 00:24:05.380
was more effective in
reducing depressive symptoms.

00:24:05.380 --> 00:24:09.870
And they reported side effects,
but they were basically saying,

00:24:09.870 --> 00:24:13.200
serious side effects
in the Paxil group,

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:16.560
in imipramine group, five,
and two in the placebo group.

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:19.680
But then there's
this effort to say,

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:22.080
let's really take a second
look at some of these trials.

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:26.070
Recently the BMJ publishes this
thing, "Restoring Study 329,

00:24:26.070 --> 00:24:30.540
Efficacy and Harms of
Paroxetine and Imipramine

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:33.060
in Treatment of Major
Depression in Adolescents."

00:24:33.060 --> 00:24:36.511
This study ends up saying
efficacy re-analysis, according

00:24:36.511 --> 00:24:38.010
to the way that
they did it, doesn't

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:39.600
look like it's
any better, but it

00:24:39.600 --> 00:24:42.920
does look like it's associated
with suicidal and self-injury

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:43.712
events.

00:24:43.712 --> 00:24:45.420
There's a lot of
concern now, that people

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:48.357
were being prescribed Paxil,
especially adolescents, to try

00:24:48.357 --> 00:24:50.190
to treat depression but
actually was raising

00:24:50.190 --> 00:24:52.324
the risk of suicidality.

00:24:52.324 --> 00:24:54.240
The point I'm making--
and I don't want to get

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:56.250
into the details
of how this is--

00:24:56.250 --> 00:24:59.430
but it's just the point that
different groups can see

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:01.502
the same data, even trial data.

00:25:01.502 --> 00:25:03.960
What I've learned to discover
is that people have this idea

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:08.400
that it's so
formulaic in a trial,

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:10.830
that you can give
anyone the same data

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:13.020
and they're going to come
up with the same answer.

00:25:13.020 --> 00:25:16.950
And any questioning
of that is again,

00:25:16.950 --> 00:25:20.791
sort of criticizing the
integrity of the investigators.

00:25:20.791 --> 00:25:22.290
And I'm trying to
take all that out.

00:25:22.290 --> 00:25:24.930
I am not criticizing either
those earlier or the late.

00:25:24.930 --> 00:25:27.690
I'm just suggesting
the possibility

00:25:27.690 --> 00:25:29.550
that even well-intentioned
people will

00:25:29.550 --> 00:25:31.500
come to different
conclusions and again, this

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:34.040
is what Brian's showing
time and time again now

00:25:34.040 --> 00:25:35.490
in the reproducibility studies.

00:25:35.490 --> 00:25:38.610
You don't have to think
that it's about bad actors.

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:41.640
I think the moment you
worry about bad actors is

00:25:41.640 --> 00:25:44.760
when you begin to
trivialize this problem,

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:48.060
because then it just becomes
about the people who don't have

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:52.020
good morals, the people who
are just seeking their own self

00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:55.040
benefit, and if we can
just identify those people

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:57.960
and get them out because
it's just like racism.

00:25:57.960 --> 00:26:00.090
It's just like
racism, I guess, which

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:03.690
is like, let's get rid of
the personalized racism

00:26:03.690 --> 00:26:06.270
but we neglect the
structural racism.

00:26:06.270 --> 00:26:09.960
It's just like saying,
because it can't be me.

00:26:09.960 --> 00:26:13.920
I can't be caught up in this
because I'm not like that.

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:17.700
And just like racism,
I'm not like that,

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:21.910
but the truth is, we're talking
about something that pervades

00:26:21.910 --> 00:26:26.020
the human experience, this
cognitive bias in this case,

00:26:26.020 --> 00:26:28.750
the way in which people think
about race and differences

00:26:28.750 --> 00:26:30.760
and tribalism in another case.

00:26:30.760 --> 00:26:32.470
These are things
that we have to begin

00:26:32.470 --> 00:26:36.940
to say are not sequestered to
a few bad actors but in fact,

00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:41.050
are affecting the entire
scientific enterprise.

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:45.040
I believe that the transparency,
the willingness to be,

00:26:45.040 --> 00:26:48.790
the sharing is the most
important treatment

00:26:48.790 --> 00:26:50.830
of that problem in science.

00:26:50.830 --> 00:26:54.270
Because it begins to open
our minds to the idea-- now,

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:56.240
I will tell you, on
things like this,

00:26:56.240 --> 00:26:58.790
what I hear from
people is, I don't

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:01.550
want to share my data because
there are people who disagree

00:27:01.550 --> 00:27:07.190
with me, who are agenda driven,
who are going to take my data,

00:27:07.190 --> 00:27:09.530
and then they're going
to do bad things with it.

00:27:09.530 --> 00:27:10.640
What are the bad things?

00:27:10.640 --> 00:27:12.056
They're going to
produce something

00:27:12.056 --> 00:27:13.780
that disagrees with me.

00:27:13.780 --> 00:27:15.070
What's implicit in that?

00:27:15.070 --> 00:27:16.450
I don't have an agenda.

00:27:16.450 --> 00:27:18.040
I don't have a point of view.

00:27:18.040 --> 00:27:19.750
I haven't done that same thing.

00:27:19.750 --> 00:27:24.040
And in my field, a lot of
the people running trials

00:27:24.040 --> 00:27:26.650
are people who have a deep
seated belief in the value

00:27:26.650 --> 00:27:27.970
of a particular treatment.

00:27:27.970 --> 00:27:30.100
They wouldn't even be
the head of the trial

00:27:30.100 --> 00:27:32.080
if they didn't, in
their hearts, say,

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:35.710
this could be something that
could be successful for people.

00:27:35.710 --> 00:27:38.949
We somehow don't have a
machine, where somebody

00:27:38.949 --> 00:27:39.990
thinks this is important.

00:27:39.990 --> 00:27:42.550
I want to find you, who don't
have an opinion about it,

00:27:42.550 --> 00:27:43.420
to study it.

00:27:43.420 --> 00:27:46.797
We tend to find people to
study it who are enthusiasts.

00:27:46.797 --> 00:27:48.880
And that's what, by the
way, gives them the energy

00:27:48.880 --> 00:27:51.234
to recruit the sites and
bring people on board

00:27:51.234 --> 00:27:52.900
and there are lots
of things about that.

00:27:52.900 --> 00:27:54.610
Then they go on
a tour afterwards

00:27:54.610 --> 00:27:57.812
and tell people why it's such
a success if it's successful.

00:27:57.812 --> 00:27:59.270
The companies who
are paying for it

00:27:59.270 --> 00:28:01.395
want people like that, not
necessarily because they

00:28:01.395 --> 00:28:03.353
want people who are going
to corrupt the trial,

00:28:03.353 --> 00:28:05.119
but they want someone
who can cheerlead

00:28:05.119 --> 00:28:06.160
if this thing's positive.

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:07.243
They want opinion leaders.

00:28:07.243 --> 00:28:09.850
They want people who actually
are excited about this idea,

00:28:09.850 --> 00:28:12.310
because they want the
translation if they

00:28:12.310 --> 00:28:14.330
get the positive effect.

00:28:14.330 --> 00:28:17.470
But what we have to begin to
realize is we all have agendas.

00:28:17.470 --> 00:28:21.310
We all have certain frames
that which we approach this,

00:28:21.310 --> 00:28:22.750
and we all need
to find ways that

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:25.060
are going to help us
get to the right answer.

00:28:25.060 --> 00:28:26.980
What that truth might
be or the understanding

00:28:26.980 --> 00:28:30.690
that truth may be murkier
than we would like to believe.

00:28:33.250 --> 00:28:37.200
So we begin to look at this
selective publication issue.

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:40.930
I'm going to now just pivot
back to the reporting issue,

00:28:40.930 --> 00:28:43.960
for example, because I'm
talking about data sharing.

00:28:43.960 --> 00:28:47.211
Actually, as I think about it, I
should have gotten to this part

00:28:47.211 --> 00:28:47.710
first.

00:28:47.710 --> 00:28:50.950
Because even before you get to
data sharing, the question is,

00:28:50.950 --> 00:28:53.321
are you telling
anybody what you found?

00:28:53.321 --> 00:28:55.070
Because for people who
are non-scientists,

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:56.980
this becomes important
because if I'm

00:28:56.980 --> 00:29:00.074
the most conscientious physician
and when you come and see me

00:29:00.074 --> 00:29:01.990
and you've got a problem,
what I'm going to do

00:29:01.990 --> 00:29:03.670
is make sure I
scour the literature

00:29:03.670 --> 00:29:08.200
to understand what's good and
bad about particular strategies

00:29:08.200 --> 00:29:09.520
for you.

00:29:09.520 --> 00:29:12.760
If people have
systematically not put

00:29:12.760 --> 00:29:14.800
information in the
literature, I'm

00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:19.900
going to have a biased view
of what's known, right?

00:29:19.900 --> 00:29:22.930
Because a lot of what's
known to some people

00:29:22.930 --> 00:29:25.270
is not being aired generally.

00:29:25.270 --> 00:29:27.190
And so I'm in a
position of weakness

00:29:27.190 --> 00:29:30.049
with regard to being
able to understand that.

00:29:30.049 --> 00:29:32.090
People have been talking
about this for a while--

00:29:32.090 --> 00:29:33.715
I don't know-- for
a while in my field.

00:29:33.715 --> 00:29:36.100
It was 1990 but
Ian Chalmers starts

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:40.489
writing about this issue,
about under-reporting research.

00:29:40.489 --> 00:29:41.530
I think I'm going to go--

00:29:44.350 --> 00:29:49.880
this is-- so one of the
ways that we developed

00:29:49.880 --> 00:29:52.430
to try to at least even
understand this problem was,

00:29:52.430 --> 00:29:56.300
we started requiring people
to register their trials.

00:29:56.300 --> 00:29:58.760
They wanted to get published
in, particularly a high impact

00:29:58.760 --> 00:30:01.460
journal, you have to
have been registered.

00:30:01.460 --> 00:30:03.950
So we can start to look
at what percent of those

00:30:03.950 --> 00:30:08.390
that are registered and
documented as completed.

00:30:08.390 --> 00:30:11.980
Let me just say that a lot
of people will register.

00:30:11.980 --> 00:30:13.870
Who the heck knows
if they complete it?

00:30:13.870 --> 00:30:14.870
They actually don't say.

00:30:14.870 --> 00:30:17.440
So assume that some of the
people who never fill out

00:30:17.440 --> 00:30:20.766
that they completed the study
actually did complete it.

00:30:20.766 --> 00:30:22.390
But if you're not
going to publish it--

00:30:22.390 --> 00:30:24.820
I mean, they didn't go back
on to the clinicaltrials.gov

00:30:24.820 --> 00:30:25.600
to do that.

00:30:25.600 --> 00:30:27.100
This is what always amazes me--

00:30:27.100 --> 00:30:29.050
the people who
actually took the time

00:30:29.050 --> 00:30:31.990
to go back onto
clinicaltrials.gov and document

00:30:31.990 --> 00:30:35.410
that it's completed
is a subset, right?

00:30:35.410 --> 00:30:38.092
You've shrunk the
denominator because I mean,

00:30:38.092 --> 00:30:40.300
the most egregious thing is
I've done this experiment

00:30:40.300 --> 00:30:42.190
and I'm just done.

00:30:42.190 --> 00:30:44.770
Why am I even going to go
back on the registration site

00:30:44.770 --> 00:30:46.090
to say that we're finished?

00:30:46.090 --> 00:30:47.710
I mean, I don't
even know why people

00:30:47.710 --> 00:30:50.210
do that if they're not going
to try to do something with it.

00:30:50.210 --> 00:30:54.720
But if you look at completed,
your rates are like 40%.

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:56.050
NIH it was low.

00:30:56.050 --> 00:30:59.190
We started producing
these curves.

00:30:59.190 --> 00:31:00.990
We published this in BMJ.

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:05.370
Percentage of studies published,
time from study completion--

00:31:05.370 --> 00:31:09.120
we're sort of topping
out here at about 65%.

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:10.560
But the other thing is time.

00:31:10.560 --> 00:31:14.550
We started realizing about
the scientific enterprise--

00:31:14.550 --> 00:31:16.950
this, again, gets to Brian's
talk about pre-prints.

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:20.569
So if you've done an
experiment, tell me

00:31:20.569 --> 00:31:22.110
how important it is
if you don't care

00:31:22.110 --> 00:31:25.280
it takes two years to actually
get it published from the time

00:31:25.280 --> 00:31:26.000
you're done.

00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:28.880
I mean, if you don't
feel any time urgency--

00:31:28.880 --> 00:31:29.450
I don't know.

00:31:29.450 --> 00:31:31.460
How important is the finding?

00:31:31.460 --> 00:31:33.320
And again, I'm talking
about medicine now.

00:31:33.320 --> 00:31:36.140
Where if [INAUDIBLE] wants to
take five years telling you

00:31:36.140 --> 00:31:40.010
he's figured out how to unlock
the secrets of the universe,

00:31:40.010 --> 00:31:45.200
he should take his time,
because it should be beautiful

00:31:45.200 --> 00:31:47.159
when he's done and elegant.

00:31:47.159 --> 00:31:48.950
For us, it's like, if
we've got information

00:31:48.950 --> 00:31:52.520
that can save lives, that can
inform choices in medicine,

00:31:52.520 --> 00:31:56.334
I mean, we've got an imperative
to kind of get that stuff out.

00:31:56.334 --> 00:31:57.500
But look where you are here?

00:31:57.500 --> 00:32:02.380
I mean, this is 20 months
and we're under 30%.

00:32:02.380 --> 00:32:09.259
40 months-- I mean,
these are huge numbers.

00:32:09.259 --> 00:32:11.425
So this is what I wanted
to show you, is publication

00:32:11.425 --> 00:32:13.090
of important clinical
trial results

00:32:13.090 --> 00:32:14.715
across academic medical centers.

00:32:14.715 --> 00:32:16.090
First, start
thinking about this.

00:32:16.090 --> 00:32:18.130
Start saying that this
is a prominent industry.

00:32:18.130 --> 00:32:22.330
Then we start saying,
it was writ large.

00:32:22.330 --> 00:32:23.680
And we start talking to deans.

00:32:23.680 --> 00:32:26.740
We went to the AAMC, the
American Association of Medical

00:32:26.740 --> 00:32:28.560
Colleges, and
said, hey, we ought

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:31.540
to hold hands and
solve this problem.

00:32:31.540 --> 00:32:34.540
And they looked
at me, like, why?

00:32:34.540 --> 00:32:37.900
They're like, there's a
lot to do here already,

00:32:37.900 --> 00:32:40.360
and we don't want to put
pressure on the medical schools

00:32:40.360 --> 00:32:43.030
to do more or the same.

00:32:43.030 --> 00:32:45.270
But I want to be
clear about these.

00:32:45.270 --> 00:32:50.990
These are clinical trials
in clinicaltrials.gov

00:32:50.990 --> 00:32:55.730
that were completed, and
we're asking within two years,

00:32:55.730 --> 00:32:58.100
what percent of
them are published?

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:01.830
Human experiments
that-- extra funding.

00:33:01.830 --> 00:33:06.526
And the best place is University
of Minnesota at about 55%.

00:33:09.192 --> 00:33:10.900
You can say whatever
you want about this,

00:33:10.900 --> 00:33:12.324
but it's all about the same.

00:33:12.324 --> 00:33:13.990
I mean, there are a
few here at the end.

00:33:13.990 --> 00:33:17.010
University of Nebraska
is really lagging.

00:33:17.010 --> 00:33:18.630
I mean, Yale's here,
but I mean, we've

00:33:18.630 --> 00:33:19.713
got nothing to brag about.

00:33:19.713 --> 00:33:21.880
We're under 50%.

00:33:21.880 --> 00:33:26.000
So we published this in BMJ.

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:28.730
I mean, I would have thought
this is a national outrage.

00:33:28.730 --> 00:33:29.645
I mean, these are--

00:33:29.645 --> 00:33:31.820
many are public institutions.

00:33:31.820 --> 00:33:34.500
They're all institutions who
are taking federal dollars.

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:38.165
They're experimenting on people,
and they're not telling anyone.

00:33:38.165 --> 00:33:41.300
And I want to just say something
about this word-- publication

00:33:41.300 --> 00:33:43.040
and reporting.

00:33:43.040 --> 00:33:45.800
Publication reporting--
what did that mean to us?

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:48.050
We said-- so the
clinicaltrials.gov now

00:33:48.050 --> 00:33:50.480
has a place where you
can report results.

00:33:50.480 --> 00:33:52.820
So it takes about 10 minutes.

00:33:52.820 --> 00:33:55.220
You go on the site,
you say this--

00:33:55.220 --> 00:33:57.800
they're not asking
for anything fancy.

00:33:57.800 --> 00:33:59.420
You had an experiment.

00:33:59.420 --> 00:34:00.620
You registered it.

00:34:00.620 --> 00:34:02.330
What was the main finding?

00:34:02.330 --> 00:34:10.039
So these rates represent they
neither reported nor published.

00:34:10.039 --> 00:34:12.080
Because some people tell
me about the publishing.

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:13.679
Well, maybe I tried to publish.

00:34:13.679 --> 00:34:14.875
It was hard to publish.

00:34:14.875 --> 00:34:15.874
I kept getting rejected.

00:34:15.874 --> 00:34:20.239
It's not my fault.
Well, I ask, how good is

00:34:20.239 --> 00:34:21.800
your research if
two years later, you

00:34:21.800 --> 00:34:24.139
can't get the experiment
on people published?

00:34:24.139 --> 00:34:26.270
I mean, that's a big problem.

00:34:26.270 --> 00:34:29.780
And you know, by the way,
we did this study on NIH.

00:34:29.780 --> 00:34:32.520
This is about the same
rate of the NIH funding.

00:34:32.520 --> 00:34:38.810
So of people getting NIH grants
to do experiments on people,

00:34:38.810 --> 00:34:41.810
only about half of them
were publishing or reporting

00:34:41.810 --> 00:34:43.770
the results within two years.

00:34:43.770 --> 00:34:45.929
So this is very
consistent with that.

00:34:45.929 --> 00:34:49.005
But we're saying, gee, none
of the academic institutions

00:34:49.005 --> 00:34:50.630
are reacting, so
let's do a report card

00:34:50.630 --> 00:34:52.370
on the academic institution.

00:34:52.370 --> 00:34:54.880
Still haven't
gotten much reaction

00:34:54.880 --> 00:34:59.670
and finally got NIH to
implement these guidelines

00:34:59.670 --> 00:35:01.140
that they articulated last year.

00:35:01.140 --> 00:35:03.406
This article helped
push that to say,

00:35:03.406 --> 00:35:05.280
we're going to start
saying to institutions--

00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:06.810
but what I want them
to say to institutions

00:35:06.810 --> 00:35:09.480
is, if you're in noncompliance--
within a year you haven't

00:35:09.480 --> 00:35:11.580
reported or
published the result,

00:35:11.580 --> 00:35:14.640
we're going to pull NIH funding
from the whole institution.

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:17.129
I mean, there's just no
excuse when it's that easy

00:35:17.129 --> 00:35:18.295
to say you're not reporting.

00:35:18.295 --> 00:35:20.760
I mean, this is the
lowest bar possible.

00:35:20.760 --> 00:35:23.250
This doesn't get to
anything Brian's doing,

00:35:23.250 --> 00:35:27.270
I mean, in the sense that that's
an aspiration for much more.

00:35:27.270 --> 00:35:29.040
This is about, are
you telling anyone

00:35:29.040 --> 00:35:30.960
what you found because
it's corrupting

00:35:30.960 --> 00:35:33.840
the entire scientific
literature.

00:35:33.840 --> 00:35:36.360
And that's what I say,
guidelines, everything,

00:35:36.360 --> 00:35:38.919
are only based on
part of the picture.

00:35:38.919 --> 00:35:40.710
And by the way, if
you're going to tell me,

00:35:40.710 --> 00:35:42.960
it doesn't matter
because these 50%

00:35:42.960 --> 00:35:46.380
never deserved to be published,
that they're no good.

00:35:46.380 --> 00:35:48.030
They shouldn't
influence anything.

00:35:48.030 --> 00:35:50.055
We're lucky that
we're not seeing them.

00:35:50.055 --> 00:35:52.680
Then I'm telling you that we've
got a whole lot of inefficiency

00:35:52.680 --> 00:35:54.330
in the health care
system, and we're

00:35:54.330 --> 00:35:57.360
placing a lot of patients at
potential risk for no reason

00:35:57.360 --> 00:35:58.170
at all.

00:35:58.170 --> 00:36:00.690
Because I guarantee you that
a good proportion of these

00:36:00.690 --> 00:36:03.930
are studies that
involve some risk.

00:36:03.930 --> 00:36:05.305
And so we've got
to rethink that.

00:36:05.305 --> 00:36:07.846
If someone wants to make that
argument, then you want to say,

00:36:07.846 --> 00:36:10.320
we're just wasting money,
time, and we're putting people

00:36:10.320 --> 00:36:11.430
at risk needlessly.

00:36:16.030 --> 00:36:19.290
This is, could we at least
acknowledge that the published

00:36:19.290 --> 00:36:19.790
data--

00:36:24.247 --> 00:36:26.330
that the published data
forms a classic bell curve

00:36:26.330 --> 00:36:29.360
when swept under the rug,
which essentially means

00:36:29.360 --> 00:36:30.620
things are missing at random.

00:36:30.620 --> 00:36:33.230
And there have been
some studies of this.

00:36:33.230 --> 00:36:36.070
This is one that was in the New
England Journal of Medicine.

00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:39.550
This guy had access to
data that was at the FDA.

00:36:39.550 --> 00:36:43.110
So he looked at all the
trials for a particular drug.

00:36:43.110 --> 00:36:45.940
In this case, he's looking
at an antidepressant.

00:36:45.940 --> 00:36:52.320
And he says, what percent
of the published literature

00:36:52.320 --> 00:36:54.910
agreed with the FDA
decision and didn't agree?

00:36:54.910 --> 00:36:56.410
And rather than go
through all this,

00:36:56.410 --> 00:36:57.951
I'll tell you the
bottom line of this

00:36:57.951 --> 00:37:00.970
was that the unpublished stuff
was mostly against the drug,

00:37:00.970 --> 00:37:02.310
were negative studies.

00:37:02.310 --> 00:37:05.230
Those were much more likely
to be swept under the rug.

00:37:05.230 --> 00:37:07.770
So the premise of
this article was,

00:37:07.770 --> 00:37:09.600
it's not missing at random.

00:37:09.600 --> 00:37:13.010
The stuff that's
missing is directional,

00:37:13.010 --> 00:37:15.510
and that's why we've got a bias.

00:37:15.510 --> 00:37:18.841
And it makes sense, but he
had access to all of the data.

00:37:18.841 --> 00:37:21.090
You've seen this Turner
article, I'm sure, many times.

00:37:21.090 --> 00:37:23.980
It's like, it's just
he had access to that.

00:37:23.980 --> 00:37:25.560
But every time we've seen this--

00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:26.490
and it makes sense--

00:37:26.490 --> 00:37:29.220
I mean, it's not going
to be missing at random.

00:37:29.220 --> 00:37:31.270
It's missing in a
directional way.

00:37:33.435 --> 00:37:34.810
I'm just going to
go through sort

00:37:34.810 --> 00:37:36.490
of what happened with
clinicaltrials.gov

00:37:36.490 --> 00:37:40.650
but I just kind of want
to go a little faster.

00:37:40.650 --> 00:37:44.760
There is this issue about even
with clinicaltrials.gov, a lot

00:37:44.760 --> 00:37:46.510
of people are registering late.

00:37:46.510 --> 00:37:47.772
And so why is late bad?

00:37:47.772 --> 00:37:49.480
Because you've already
started the study,

00:37:49.480 --> 00:37:50.760
maybe even the
results are coming in,

00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:53.093
or you're learning something,
the whole point about this

00:37:53.093 --> 00:37:54.660
is pre-specification.

00:37:54.660 --> 00:37:57.912
What are you going to do so we
can see at the end, did you do

00:37:57.912 --> 00:37:59.370
what you said you
were going to do,

00:37:59.370 --> 00:38:02.040
or were you starting to
change what you're looking at

00:38:02.040 --> 00:38:04.020
based on what the results were?

00:38:07.500 --> 00:38:13.710
I'm just gonna-- I know we're
kind of running late here.

00:38:13.710 --> 00:38:18.840
And actually when they
put this results reporting

00:38:18.840 --> 00:38:21.774
in clinicaltrials.gov,
the idea was

00:38:21.774 --> 00:38:23.190
that people could
have a quick way

00:38:23.190 --> 00:38:25.170
to let people know
what the findings were,

00:38:25.170 --> 00:38:27.570
even as they were waiting
for the publication.

00:38:27.570 --> 00:38:30.090
But studies after study are
saying people aren't doing it.

00:38:30.090 --> 00:38:31.548
They're not taking
advantage of it.

00:38:31.548 --> 00:38:34.620
So even though it's
mandated, it's not happening.

00:38:34.620 --> 00:38:37.080
That's part of what
the NIH is also

00:38:37.080 --> 00:38:38.580
starting to put into place.

00:38:38.580 --> 00:38:41.010
But industry, if
anything, is doing better

00:38:41.010 --> 00:38:45.300
than everyone else, and I
think most investigators

00:38:45.300 --> 00:38:48.270
aren't even tuned into this.

00:38:48.270 --> 00:38:52.800
It's sort of like, if you're not
reporting results after a year

00:38:52.800 --> 00:38:55.080
that you've finished,
there's something wrong.

00:38:55.080 --> 00:38:58.950
I mean, there's something wrong.

00:38:58.950 --> 00:39:00.150
But overall this is really--

00:39:03.811 --> 00:39:05.060
So what we were trying to do--

00:39:05.060 --> 00:39:06.518
I'd say what our
contribution was--

00:39:06.518 --> 00:39:07.730
that we started thinking--

00:39:07.730 --> 00:39:08.590
I don't know.

00:39:08.590 --> 00:39:10.542
We're kind of like 1/100
of what Brian's done.

00:39:10.542 --> 00:39:12.500
You know, we started
thinking like, well, there

00:39:12.500 --> 00:39:13.777
needs to be some solutions.

00:39:13.777 --> 00:39:15.610
What could we, at least,
contribute to this?

00:39:15.610 --> 00:39:17.240
It's not enough that
we're continuing

00:39:17.240 --> 00:39:19.700
to write papers that are
describing the problem.

00:39:19.700 --> 00:39:22.080
We need to try to think
about some solution.

00:39:22.080 --> 00:39:28.320
So we started writing, you
know, papers about data sharing,

00:39:28.320 --> 00:39:30.290
and we started looking
for an opportunity

00:39:30.290 --> 00:39:33.500
to try to create a platform
that would promote it.

00:39:33.500 --> 00:39:35.130
So the story goes
something like this.

00:39:35.130 --> 00:39:38.930
You know, I saw that Medtronic
was getting in trouble

00:39:38.930 --> 00:39:42.340
with one of its devices.

00:39:42.340 --> 00:39:44.870
A group of spine
surgeons were beginning

00:39:44.870 --> 00:39:49.670
to question the safety
of a very popular,

00:39:49.670 --> 00:39:54.530
very highly sold device
for back surgery.

00:39:54.530 --> 00:39:57.690
And we went to Medtronic
and we said, here

00:39:57.690 --> 00:39:58.940
can be an opportunity for you.

00:39:58.940 --> 00:40:01.670
If you really
believe that they're

00:40:01.670 --> 00:40:03.890
misinterpreting the
data, why don't we

00:40:03.890 --> 00:40:05.299
just release your data?

00:40:05.299 --> 00:40:07.340
And you know, Medtronic
was sort of in a position

00:40:07.340 --> 00:40:10.760
where they were preparing
to defend the complaints

00:40:10.760 --> 00:40:15.020
and to continue to sort of parry
it and do their usual PR stuff.

00:40:15.020 --> 00:40:17.792
And I think that we sort of
prevailed on them to say,

00:40:17.792 --> 00:40:19.250
why not make a
partnership with us,

00:40:19.250 --> 00:40:20.417
and let us release the data?

00:40:20.417 --> 00:40:23.041
And we actually said, if you're
worried, let's do it like this.

00:40:23.041 --> 00:40:24.040
Give us the data.

00:40:24.040 --> 00:40:28.160
We'll contract with two
of the most prominent

00:40:28.160 --> 00:40:30.909
systematic review
groups in the world.

00:40:30.909 --> 00:40:32.450
We'll give them the
data, let them do

00:40:32.450 --> 00:40:35.600
their report, their analysis
and interpretation of the data.

00:40:35.600 --> 00:40:38.149
We'll do two because we believe
that it's necessary to have

00:40:38.149 --> 00:40:38.940
two groups looking.

00:40:38.940 --> 00:40:40.250
We'll see about
their concordance.

00:40:40.250 --> 00:40:42.124
By the way, they didn't
exactly agree either.

00:40:42.124 --> 00:40:44.520
That's another paper
we've just submitted,

00:40:44.520 --> 00:40:46.370
which is trying to show
that these two world

00:40:46.370 --> 00:40:48.350
famous groups given the same
data and the same charge

00:40:48.350 --> 00:40:50.840
and same budget actually come
up with a little different

00:40:50.840 --> 00:40:52.280
conclusions.

00:40:52.280 --> 00:40:53.900
They were both
published side-by-side

00:40:53.900 --> 00:40:56.750
in the Annals of
Internal Medicine.

00:40:56.750 --> 00:40:58.850
Then we made the data
available broadly.

00:40:58.850 --> 00:41:02.030
And we said to people, we were
getting paid by Medtronic,

00:41:02.030 --> 00:41:04.160
but we had these two
independent groups,

00:41:04.160 --> 00:41:06.566
but we were still paying
them to do analysis.

00:41:06.566 --> 00:41:08.690
And then we said, but if
anyone doesn't believe it,

00:41:08.690 --> 00:41:12.230
we're giving the data away so
you can repeat it yourself.

00:41:12.230 --> 00:41:14.480
And what we were trying to
set this up so that anybody

00:41:14.480 --> 00:41:15.479
can take a look at this.

00:41:19.815 --> 00:41:23.100
We ended up creating the YODA
project, the Yale Open Data

00:41:23.100 --> 00:41:25.680
Access project,
which was a platform

00:41:25.680 --> 00:41:29.630
to try to enable people.

00:41:29.630 --> 00:41:31.380
We thought about
industry but also others,

00:41:31.380 --> 00:41:36.840
to be able to have a way
for their data to be shared

00:41:36.840 --> 00:41:39.060
but it was still attendant
to the concern of the data

00:41:39.060 --> 00:41:39.480
holders.

00:41:39.480 --> 00:41:41.938
And the kind of concerns of
the data holders that we wanted

00:41:41.938 --> 00:41:43.830
to attend to were, they
wanted to make sure

00:41:43.830 --> 00:41:45.660
that these were
research questions that

00:41:45.660 --> 00:41:50.270
were being asked, particularly
industry, not litigation, not

00:41:50.270 --> 00:41:52.470
commercial, not competitors.

00:41:52.470 --> 00:41:55.380
Although, lawyers
and competitors

00:41:55.380 --> 00:41:59.310
could put in proposals, but that
wouldn't have been ostensibly

00:41:59.310 --> 00:42:01.351
the prime motivation for it.

00:42:01.351 --> 00:42:02.850
We would evaluate
that with science.

00:42:02.850 --> 00:42:04.260
We'd help work with the people.

00:42:04.260 --> 00:42:07.740
In transparency, we
would post the requests

00:42:07.740 --> 00:42:12.650
and we would require people
to report their results.

00:42:12.650 --> 00:42:15.480
And then we would give
them access to the data.

00:42:15.480 --> 00:42:17.100
And on our side,
what was important

00:42:17.100 --> 00:42:19.230
us was we were
totally independent.

00:42:19.230 --> 00:42:21.090
Our contract with
the companies had

00:42:21.090 --> 00:42:24.060
to say we had full
decision making power

00:42:24.060 --> 00:42:26.160
over who got access
to the data, and they

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:29.670
knew that our stance was we
wanted everyone to have access.

00:42:29.670 --> 00:42:31.610
That was, we didn't
want to be a barrier.

00:42:31.610 --> 00:42:33.360
And so actually, we
haven't denied anyone.

00:42:33.360 --> 00:42:35.068
There have been a few
that we passed back

00:42:35.068 --> 00:42:40.054
because we said it's a
difficult to understand proposal

00:42:40.054 --> 00:42:41.720
and you need to work
on it a little bit.

00:42:41.720 --> 00:42:43.928
But not because we were
judging the science to be bad

00:42:43.928 --> 00:42:46.550
as much as they were having
trouble articulating exactly

00:42:46.550 --> 00:42:47.940
even what they were doing.

00:42:47.940 --> 00:42:51.800
So we were willing
to work with them,

00:42:51.800 --> 00:42:54.001
but we were trying to help
them to figure out what

00:42:54.001 --> 00:42:55.250
it was that they wanted to do.

00:42:55.250 --> 00:42:57.590
But so we created this as a way.

00:42:57.590 --> 00:42:59.770
We start with Medtronic.

00:42:59.770 --> 00:43:02.570
Medtronic actually peels
off it after two years,

00:43:02.570 --> 00:43:05.430
but we were able to enlist
Johnson and Johnson.

00:43:05.430 --> 00:43:08.330
And Johnson and Johnson
basically told us they were

00:43:08.330 --> 00:43:10.340
committed to sharing
all their trials--

00:43:10.340 --> 00:43:13.130
they're the largest drug and
device company in the world--

00:43:13.130 --> 00:43:18.200
all of their drug trials, all
of their device trials-- which

00:43:18.200 --> 00:43:20.000
was unprecedented--
and actually,

00:43:20.000 --> 00:43:25.010
their consumer products,
so Tylenol, Listerine,

00:43:25.010 --> 00:43:26.390
anything you're interested in.

00:43:26.390 --> 00:43:30.440
And we have 180 of the
trials now on the Yale site.

00:43:30.440 --> 00:43:32.960
And actually, for
anyone listening,

00:43:32.960 --> 00:43:35.030
I've always felt
that at Yale it's

00:43:35.030 --> 00:43:36.640
an under utilized resource.

00:43:36.640 --> 00:43:40.940
We have 180 trials
that anybody here

00:43:40.940 --> 00:43:44.840
could request access to to
answer a scientific question.

00:43:44.840 --> 00:43:46.940
And then you would get--

00:43:46.940 --> 00:43:50.030
it's held in a SaS
enclave, but you'd

00:43:50.030 --> 00:43:51.410
be given permission to use it.

00:43:51.410 --> 00:43:55.490
We have the metadata
about the trials.

00:43:55.490 --> 00:43:59.750
The data is de-identified and
anyone can make use of it.

00:44:04.790 --> 00:44:07.090
There have been many groups
that have looked at this.

00:44:07.090 --> 00:44:07.590
Let's see.

00:44:07.590 --> 00:44:10.910
I wanted to-- and since
we've started doing this,

00:44:10.910 --> 00:44:13.496
a lot of the other places have--

00:44:13.496 --> 00:44:15.620
the industry's been way
ahead of academics in doing

00:44:15.620 --> 00:44:19.610
this, particularly GSK.

00:44:19.610 --> 00:44:24.500
But I think that, just to
be clear, it's not our data.

00:44:24.500 --> 00:44:27.200
So we're an independent third
party without interests,

00:44:27.200 --> 00:44:29.840
removing perception
of influence access.

00:44:29.840 --> 00:44:32.210
We have full jurisdiction
to make the decisions,

00:44:32.210 --> 00:44:35.990
and we actually use public
comment to form our approach.

00:44:35.990 --> 00:44:37.607
We wanted to see
what people said,

00:44:37.607 --> 00:44:39.440
what they thought was
going to be important.

00:44:39.440 --> 00:44:44.214
If you go on our site, you'll
see categories of trials.

00:44:44.214 --> 00:44:45.380
You can go into any of them.

00:44:45.380 --> 00:44:47.510
There's a lot of
information about it,

00:44:47.510 --> 00:44:52.320
and then, we try to approve
requests as quickly as we can.

00:44:56.530 --> 00:44:59.580
All we ask-- investigator name,
affiliation, co-investigators,

00:44:59.580 --> 00:45:01.890
research proposal, including
background, study design,

00:45:01.890 --> 00:45:04.010
main outcomes, statistical
analysis, plan,

00:45:04.010 --> 00:45:05.190
and a COI statement.

00:45:05.190 --> 00:45:08.340
But all of it's for really
transparency and disclosure,

00:45:08.340 --> 00:45:11.700
and we don't dig deep
to start to-- it's not

00:45:11.700 --> 00:45:15.570
an NIH application, where
we're trying to reject anyone.

00:45:15.570 --> 00:45:17.290
We're just trying to do this.

00:45:17.290 --> 00:45:20.820
And once they're approved,
people sign a DUA.

00:45:20.820 --> 00:45:23.370
Like I said, they gain
access via mainframe

00:45:23.370 --> 00:45:26.330
and it just basically
prevents distribution

00:45:26.330 --> 00:45:29.190
and makes sure that we're
protecting patient privacy.

00:45:29.190 --> 00:45:31.350
The number of requests has
been relatively modest.

00:45:31.350 --> 00:45:35.980
I mean, we're out here, and
we have had 55 requests total.

00:45:35.980 --> 00:45:40.290
And so we think that
the principal issue here

00:45:40.290 --> 00:45:42.300
and the criticism
we're getting is

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:44.820
all of this work is
being spent on open data

00:45:44.820 --> 00:45:48.090
access but not many people
are making use of it.

00:45:48.090 --> 00:45:49.540
So is there really the demand?

00:45:49.540 --> 00:45:53.192
And we think we're still early
in this, that people are still

00:45:53.192 --> 00:45:55.150
getting used to the idea
that they can do this.

00:45:55.150 --> 00:45:57.270
That even as we've
tried to publicize this,

00:45:57.270 --> 00:46:00.210
I don't think enough people
know about the resource,

00:46:00.210 --> 00:46:04.150
and we're still deeply
committed to the idea

00:46:04.150 --> 00:46:06.336
that this is going to
yield important studies.

00:46:06.336 --> 00:46:07.960
We had our first
couple of publications

00:46:07.960 --> 00:46:09.220
from the data sharing.

00:46:09.220 --> 00:46:12.490
We think presumably
we'll see a lot more,

00:46:12.490 --> 00:46:14.740
and we're eager to sort of see.

00:46:14.740 --> 00:46:16.544
If you go on our
site, you can see

00:46:16.544 --> 00:46:18.460
we have public reporting
of approved requests,

00:46:18.460 --> 00:46:19.750
submitted proposals.

00:46:19.750 --> 00:46:20.600
This is interesting.

00:46:20.600 --> 00:46:23.530
Some of the investigators
have been concerned that we're

00:46:23.530 --> 00:46:24.970
sharing their request.

00:46:24.970 --> 00:46:27.880
That this is-- they've got the
secret to the universe, which

00:46:27.880 --> 00:46:29.338
they should share
with [INAUDIBLE],

00:46:29.338 --> 00:46:33.250
but that this is somehow
preempting their work.

00:46:33.250 --> 00:46:34.870
You know, we believe
that, again, this

00:46:34.870 --> 00:46:38.470
is an opportunity for people
to both be able to collaborate

00:46:38.470 --> 00:46:39.790
and to see what's going on.

00:46:39.790 --> 00:46:43.900
We do try to respect
concerns, but we tell them,

00:46:43.900 --> 00:46:45.620
we think we need to go.

00:46:45.620 --> 00:46:48.550
And so our goals, I
think, going forward

00:46:48.550 --> 00:46:51.040
are facilitate greater access
to clinical trial data,

00:46:51.040 --> 00:46:53.200
increase transparency by
accelerating generation

00:46:53.200 --> 00:46:56.247
of new knowledge, and at
the same time, promoting

00:46:56.247 --> 00:46:57.580
responsible conduct of research.

00:46:57.580 --> 00:46:59.080
Our whole thing
about getting people

00:46:59.080 --> 00:47:01.360
to fill it out and post
it, we believe strongly

00:47:01.360 --> 00:47:03.040
in pre-specification.

00:47:03.040 --> 00:47:04.930
Even if you're doing
exploratory analysis--

00:47:04.930 --> 00:47:06.310
this is the point I
brought up earlier--

00:47:06.310 --> 00:47:07.930
it's OK to do
exploratory analysis.

00:47:07.930 --> 00:47:10.580
Just say that's
what you're doing.

00:47:10.580 --> 00:47:13.400
And so we think we can
contribute to that.

00:47:13.400 --> 00:47:15.630
The whole goal is better
inform patients, clinicians,

00:47:15.630 --> 00:47:16.419
and industry.

00:47:16.419 --> 00:47:17.960
So decisions can be
based on the most

00:47:17.960 --> 00:47:20.000
comprehensive and contemporary
evidence available relevant

00:47:20.000 --> 00:47:21.440
to benefits and
harms of therapy.

00:47:21.440 --> 00:47:23.606
People can now go on our
site and see all the trials

00:47:23.606 --> 00:47:25.130
that Johnson and Johnson did.

00:47:25.130 --> 00:47:27.590
Published or unpublished,
you can see them,

00:47:27.590 --> 00:47:30.650
and people can make the
choices for themselves.

00:47:30.650 --> 00:47:34.880
So we like the Yoda
theme, you know?

00:47:34.880 --> 00:47:35.460
Try not.

00:47:35.460 --> 00:47:36.300
Do or do not.

00:47:36.300 --> 00:47:37.970
There is no try.

00:47:37.970 --> 00:47:39.740
I think the point--

00:47:39.740 --> 00:47:44.390
I don't understand what
that means but the point is,

00:47:44.390 --> 00:47:48.320
I think, for us in academia,
the responsibility is not just

00:47:48.320 --> 00:47:51.290
to describe problems but
try to solve problems.

00:47:51.290 --> 00:47:54.855
And in this case, if we think
that there are approaches,

00:47:54.855 --> 00:47:55.730
we should experiment.

00:47:55.730 --> 00:47:57.050
We should try them.

00:47:57.050 --> 00:48:02.030
And one of my hopes, I
think, is that at Yale, we

00:48:02.030 --> 00:48:04.640
can begin to get more of the
faculty interested in trying

00:48:04.640 --> 00:48:09.440
to set an example about what
good science looks like--

00:48:09.440 --> 00:48:11.870
not just good science
in terms of publication

00:48:11.870 --> 00:48:15.200
and high-profile places and lots
of publicity, but good science,

00:48:15.200 --> 00:48:17.420
in terms of the way
it's done, the workings.

00:48:17.420 --> 00:48:19.700
How we teach our students
about what that is.

00:48:19.700 --> 00:48:21.960
What's the ethos that we have?

00:48:21.960 --> 00:48:23.870
And how we can reflect
a lot more people

00:48:23.870 --> 00:48:26.420
like Aaron, who maybe are
going to say that this

00:48:26.420 --> 00:48:28.250
is about a social good.

00:48:28.250 --> 00:48:31.250
And that we need to
have a sort of spirit

00:48:31.250 --> 00:48:33.890
here that we're trying to
be open and transparent,

00:48:33.890 --> 00:48:36.230
and we want others
to join us, and we

00:48:36.230 --> 00:48:38.132
think that's in
everyone's best interest.

00:48:38.132 --> 00:48:39.090
So thank you very much.

00:48:49.539 --> 00:48:51.080
Let's take some
questions for Harlan.

00:48:59.720 --> 00:49:00.330
Thank you.

00:49:00.330 --> 00:49:02.730
Just a quick question--

00:49:02.730 --> 00:49:04.950
do you see the setup you
have right now for YODA

00:49:04.950 --> 00:49:08.126
as eventually being unnecessary.

00:49:08.126 --> 00:49:10.500
So is there going to be a
point where the data could just

00:49:10.500 --> 00:49:14.910
be released, or do you foresee
always having an intermediary,

00:49:14.910 --> 00:49:16.630
someone who makes an approval.

00:49:16.630 --> 00:49:18.270
I think it's really
a good question.

00:49:18.270 --> 00:49:20.010
We have, I think,
taken the position

00:49:20.010 --> 00:49:23.550
that we should not be thinking
about our self-perpetuation

00:49:23.550 --> 00:49:25.460
as being one of our goals.

00:49:25.460 --> 00:49:30.009
Now, but we do think we still
fill an important purpose.

00:49:30.009 --> 00:49:32.550
And the question will be whether
this will all come together,

00:49:32.550 --> 00:49:34.290
because what people
actually want to do

00:49:34.290 --> 00:49:38.300
is be able to combine trials
across companies, too.

00:49:38.300 --> 00:49:42.150
And so right now, J and J will
have their stuff in an enclave.

00:49:42.150 --> 00:49:43.632
GSK has their stuff
in an enclave.

00:49:43.632 --> 00:49:45.215
And the question
will be, can we start

00:49:45.215 --> 00:49:47.520
to create an omnibus
system so that if I'm

00:49:47.520 --> 00:49:50.010
pulling a trial from
three different companies,

00:49:50.010 --> 00:49:52.290
I can actually have
a place to put them

00:49:52.290 --> 00:49:54.630
and they're all permitting
that kind of combo.

00:49:54.630 --> 00:49:57.030
I will tell you, J and
J is open to that now.

00:49:57.030 --> 00:50:00.210
But it's not optimal
that YODA exists

00:50:00.210 --> 00:50:02.350
and several others exist.

00:50:02.350 --> 00:50:05.450
We do think that the
independence is important.

00:50:05.450 --> 00:50:07.775
And when the companies are
kind of hiring the people

00:50:07.775 --> 00:50:09.150
and they're their
consultants who

00:50:09.150 --> 00:50:11.080
are deciding who gets access.

00:50:11.080 --> 00:50:11.890
That's not ideal.

00:50:11.890 --> 00:50:15.100
We have requested data
from some of the others,

00:50:15.100 --> 00:50:18.390
and it's a more cumbersome
process than what we've got.

00:50:18.390 --> 00:50:21.215
And I think if they can
move in that direction,

00:50:21.215 --> 00:50:23.590
and academia can move, maybe
we can find some other form.

00:50:23.590 --> 00:50:26.040
Maybe YODA goes away.

00:50:26.040 --> 00:50:30.460
You know, it's not our vision
that it has to be the solution.

00:50:30.460 --> 00:50:32.470
What we were trying to
do is say that there

00:50:32.470 --> 00:50:35.260
are certain properties of
what YODA has that whatever,

00:50:35.260 --> 00:50:37.600
you know, we get to
finally, should probably

00:50:37.600 --> 00:50:38.860
be part of that process.

00:50:38.860 --> 00:50:41.200
We're happy to serve
the purpose but again,

00:50:41.200 --> 00:50:44.590
you know, being
in an institution,

00:50:44.590 --> 00:50:46.280
we've also got some
limitations as well.

00:50:46.280 --> 00:50:47.220
It may be that--

00:50:47.220 --> 00:50:49.761
maybe it should, you know,
become part of the Center

00:50:49.761 --> 00:50:51.010
for Open Science or something.

00:50:51.010 --> 00:50:52.968
Maybe there's another
place where it ultimately

00:50:52.968 --> 00:50:55.515
sits that becomes kind of
an independent place for it.

00:50:55.515 --> 00:50:56.485
I don't know.

00:51:02.046 --> 00:51:03.670
Everyone's tired at
the end of the day.

00:51:03.670 --> 00:51:04.270
That's OK.

00:51:08.370 --> 00:51:12.560
There's one sense
in which the 80%

00:51:12.560 --> 00:51:15.830
is completely terrifying
about what isn't being done

00:51:15.830 --> 00:51:18.180
with open science and medicine.

00:51:18.180 --> 00:51:19.860
But there is
another sense where,

00:51:19.860 --> 00:51:23.400
it seems to me, that the
breadth of work that you've done

00:51:23.400 --> 00:51:26.640
is a illustration of
victory of open science.

00:51:26.640 --> 00:51:30.330
Because just one
example, the rates

00:51:30.330 --> 00:51:35.730
of reporting studies that have
been completed after 24 months,

00:51:35.730 --> 00:51:38.610
there is no way to do that
study in preclinical or basic

00:51:38.610 --> 00:51:39.390
sciences.

00:51:39.390 --> 00:51:41.190
It's just not possible to do it.

00:51:41.190 --> 00:51:43.200
We have no idea what
studies have been done.

00:51:43.200 --> 00:51:45.810
There is no registry of
what studies are being done.

00:51:48.330 --> 00:51:50.762
There is, even
despite, oh, my god,

00:51:50.762 --> 00:51:51.970
there's a lot happening here.

00:51:51.970 --> 00:51:54.330
This seems very
optimistic in that there

00:51:54.330 --> 00:51:57.750
are ways to make it serviceable
so that you can identify it.

00:51:57.750 --> 00:52:01.540
You can do research on the
process, figure fi ways

00:52:01.540 --> 00:52:02.610
and improve it.

00:52:02.610 --> 00:52:07.009
So do you see--

00:52:07.009 --> 00:52:08.800
are you cynical or
optimistic about where--

00:52:08.800 --> 00:52:13.410
No, really, I mean,
we're actually, I think--

00:52:13.410 --> 00:52:16.020
to have lived through this
period, where we first

00:52:16.020 --> 00:52:18.210
saw a problem and
no one was really

00:52:18.210 --> 00:52:21.810
working on it in medicine, to a
point where I've got, you know,

00:52:21.810 --> 00:52:24.642
Rob Califf, as an
FDA commissioner,

00:52:24.642 --> 00:52:26.100
standing up and
saying open science

00:52:26.100 --> 00:52:27.600
is the only way to
go going forward.

00:52:27.600 --> 00:52:29.670
And Rob's kind of convert.

00:52:29.670 --> 00:52:32.130
You know, he was running
the Duke Clinical Research

00:52:32.130 --> 00:52:33.600
Institute and founded it.

00:52:33.600 --> 00:52:35.325
He's a great investigator.

00:52:35.325 --> 00:52:37.950
But you know, Rob was one of the
early people when I talked to,

00:52:37.950 --> 00:52:41.080
was kind of, you know,
not sure about it.

00:52:41.080 --> 00:52:44.360
And you know, what I see now
is, you go to our meetings

00:52:44.360 --> 00:52:47.480
and you can--

00:52:47.480 --> 00:52:49.330
where it's about the
edges now, you know?

00:52:49.330 --> 00:52:51.070
How many months should
it be, or how much time

00:52:51.070 --> 00:52:51.861
should we be given?

00:52:51.861 --> 00:52:55.330
And some of the trialists are
still concerned about the work

00:52:55.330 --> 00:52:58.330
they've put in and what they're
getting now but there's a--

00:52:58.330 --> 00:52:59.650
we've moved at light speed.

00:52:59.650 --> 00:53:02.560
I mean, to be able to
get this from the NIH.

00:53:02.560 --> 00:53:04.805
To get what Gates is
saying, which is your stuff

00:53:04.805 --> 00:53:05.680
should go right away.

00:53:05.680 --> 00:53:08.230
To get what Cory's going
to say, which is you've

00:53:08.230 --> 00:53:10.210
got to be able to report
your stuff rapidly,

00:53:10.210 --> 00:53:15.370
and we expect the data to be
shared from high stakes funded

00:53:15.370 --> 00:53:16.060
projects.

00:53:16.060 --> 00:53:19.690
I mean, from a time when
nobody was thinking like this,

00:53:19.690 --> 00:53:22.870
it's only about 15 years,
10 or 15 years, it's--

00:53:22.870 --> 00:53:23.800
maybe 10 years really.

00:53:23.800 --> 00:53:25.240
It's really a remarkable change.

00:53:25.240 --> 00:53:26.612
So I think we're excited.

00:53:26.612 --> 00:53:28.570
That's why we're excited
to work with you, too.

00:53:28.570 --> 00:53:30.940
I mean, we think there are
other frontiers out here,

00:53:30.940 --> 00:53:32.570
but I think we're getting there.

00:53:32.570 --> 00:53:38.650
And I think it will be at
a point where, you know,

00:53:38.650 --> 00:53:40.870
it'll seem to be unbelievable
that somebody would not

00:53:40.870 --> 00:53:43.597
be reporting their information
And that's what we want.

00:53:43.597 --> 00:53:45.430
You know, we don't want
to embarrass people.

00:53:45.430 --> 00:53:46.680
It's not about shaming people.

00:53:46.680 --> 00:53:48.730
We want to create
normative behaviors, where

00:53:48.730 --> 00:53:50.319
that's just what's expected.

00:53:50.319 --> 00:53:51.610
And that's what we're teaching.

00:53:51.610 --> 00:53:52.630
That's what we model.

00:53:52.630 --> 00:53:53.790
That's what we do.

00:53:53.790 --> 00:53:57.220
And if you don't do it, there's
something wrong, you know?

00:53:57.220 --> 00:53:59.090
Meaning that, you
know, it's an anomaly.

00:53:59.090 --> 00:54:01.130
Something's at issue.

00:54:01.130 --> 00:54:02.290
And we're getting closer.

00:54:02.290 --> 00:54:04.980
So I think if you hear
impatience in my voice,

00:54:04.980 --> 00:54:07.520
it's just because I want
to see change faster,

00:54:07.520 --> 00:54:11.140
but in terms of progress,
it's been remarkable, really

00:54:11.140 --> 00:54:12.126
remarkable.

00:54:21.550 --> 00:54:22.930
Thanks.

00:54:22.930 --> 00:54:28.810
I have a comment about some of
the non-reproducible results

00:54:28.810 --> 00:54:34.120
in clinical trials, maybe
psychology experiments, too.

00:54:34.120 --> 00:54:36.580
So in a lot of
these experiments,

00:54:36.580 --> 00:54:39.190
the variability in
subjects gets reduced down

00:54:39.190 --> 00:54:43.570
to really discrete demographic
variables like age and sex

00:54:43.570 --> 00:54:48.250
and race, but human
biology and personality

00:54:48.250 --> 00:54:51.140
is much more complex than that.

00:54:51.140 --> 00:54:55.120
So in some sense, even
if you are perfectly

00:54:55.120 --> 00:55:00.280
replicating results and you
have appropriate power based

00:55:00.280 --> 00:55:02.890
on the variability that
you're modeling for,

00:55:02.890 --> 00:55:06.850
you expect that there's maybe
just a ton more random error

00:55:06.850 --> 00:55:08.200
in subjects.

00:55:08.200 --> 00:55:10.990
And so if you accept
that, we really

00:55:10.990 --> 00:55:14.800
have to replicate
studies multiple times,

00:55:14.800 --> 00:55:17.890
not to validate the
results of one study

00:55:17.890 --> 00:55:20.560
but because only through
multiple replication could

00:55:20.560 --> 00:55:22.870
you ever come at some
kind of consensus

00:55:22.870 --> 00:55:25.030
given variable outcomes,
that, yes, this

00:55:25.030 --> 00:55:26.960
is a real effect or not.

00:55:26.960 --> 00:55:31.300
So there's still important
room for open science and data

00:55:31.300 --> 00:55:34.030
sharing in all that but
more of an incentive

00:55:34.030 --> 00:55:38.020
actually for multiple labs
to be replicating results

00:55:38.020 --> 00:55:42.440
and then come up with one
consensus after those studies.

00:55:42.440 --> 00:55:44.947
I think our biggest challenge
is the cost and time that's

00:55:44.947 --> 00:55:46.780
involved in doing any
of the single studies.

00:55:46.780 --> 00:55:50.850
It's like, it's a big
mountain to climb.

00:55:50.850 --> 00:55:53.600
Physicists, you know, have
the same issues, you know?

00:55:53.600 --> 00:55:56.040
That they can't create--

00:55:56.040 --> 00:55:57.700
Cerno creates something
big in France.

00:55:57.700 --> 00:55:58.850
They can't create it
all over the place.

00:55:58.850 --> 00:55:59.590
So they've got to do it right.

00:55:59.590 --> 00:56:01.760
For us, though, there are all
these different variables.

00:56:01.760 --> 00:56:02.990
So there's two issues
you brought up.

00:56:02.990 --> 00:56:04.281
One is about external validity.

00:56:04.281 --> 00:56:07.180
I mean, it's valid
for that if you

00:56:07.180 --> 00:56:08.800
do believe the results
for that group,

00:56:08.800 --> 00:56:11.830
is it equally valid for older
people or younger people

00:56:11.830 --> 00:56:15.967
or people in different countries
or in different health systems?

00:56:15.967 --> 00:56:17.800
And then there's the
issue of whether or not

00:56:17.800 --> 00:56:20.710
there is actually internal
validity and whether or not

00:56:20.710 --> 00:56:24.140
actually what you found in
that group could be replicated

00:56:24.140 --> 00:56:26.950
again, and that's really--

00:56:26.950 --> 00:56:28.941
that relationship is true.

00:56:28.941 --> 00:56:30.940
And then you've got the
stochastic nature of it,

00:56:30.940 --> 00:56:33.070
too, which is if you
did it enough times,

00:56:33.070 --> 00:56:35.680
you're going to see some
distribution of effect.

00:56:35.680 --> 00:56:37.360
Because it's likely
not deterministic,

00:56:37.360 --> 00:56:40.300
where you give everyone this
and you give a and b happens.

00:56:40.300 --> 00:56:43.600
It's you increase the
likelihood of a or b happening.

00:56:43.600 --> 00:56:46.630
And so the distance
between the two groups,

00:56:46.630 --> 00:56:49.020
even within the
same group, there's

00:56:49.020 --> 00:56:51.940
going to be some
distribution of effect sizes

00:56:51.940 --> 00:56:54.890
that, you know, just
by chance alone.

00:56:54.890 --> 00:56:57.640
So I mean, that's what
makes it complicated.

00:56:57.640 --> 00:56:58.560
But I don't know.

00:56:58.560 --> 00:57:00.643
Brian, you've thought more
about this than anyone,

00:57:00.643 --> 00:57:02.230
about the approach.

00:57:02.230 --> 00:57:05.630
Because in a lot of these
cases that aren't replicated,

00:57:05.630 --> 00:57:09.820
you've actually held constant
a lot of the other issues.

00:57:09.820 --> 00:57:12.564
But once you start
saying whether it would--

00:57:12.564 --> 00:57:14.980
whether when you don't see it
replicated in another group,

00:57:14.980 --> 00:57:17.335
that represents an
interaction or it

00:57:17.335 --> 00:57:20.710
represents a non-replication
of the initial study.

00:57:20.710 --> 00:57:23.800
Those are two hypotheses
that have to be further,

00:57:23.800 --> 00:57:25.979
I think, evaluated.

00:57:25.979 --> 00:57:26.895
They're both possible.

00:57:29.730 --> 00:57:33.225
I don't know if you have
anything, Brian, on that.

00:57:33.225 --> 00:57:33.850
Other comments?

00:57:37.180 --> 00:57:37.710
Thanks.

00:57:37.710 --> 00:57:42.940
So I have questions about
the platform on data sharing

00:57:42.940 --> 00:57:44.260
and [INAUDIBLE] of data.

00:57:44.260 --> 00:57:49.480
So I work in VA CSP, so we
have done a lot of trials.

00:57:49.480 --> 00:57:51.760
We have discussed this as
an internal discussion,

00:57:51.760 --> 00:57:54.860
about data collection
procedures.

00:57:54.860 --> 00:57:59.160
So we found that most of the
time-- well, so for example,

00:57:59.160 --> 00:58:02.610
for defining a certain
disease, the different ICD

00:58:02.610 --> 00:58:06.310
codes, or if you define,
for example, hypokalemia,

00:58:06.310 --> 00:58:09.880
whether you based on lab
results or some trials

00:58:09.880 --> 00:58:13.570
require you come back in
one or two weeks to confirm.

00:58:13.570 --> 00:58:15.220
In that case, a
lot of details that

00:58:15.220 --> 00:58:17.230
will affect how
the data is really

00:58:17.230 --> 00:58:19.630
compared, whether
it's in a fair way.

00:58:19.630 --> 00:58:23.080
So I wonder when you have
that data sharing projects,

00:58:23.080 --> 00:58:27.790
do you have some plan to have,
for example, like protocols

00:58:27.790 --> 00:58:30.100
or operational
manuals that will help

00:58:30.100 --> 00:58:33.880
people to really understand
how the data is collected.

00:58:33.880 --> 00:58:35.440
So this is an important point.

00:58:35.440 --> 00:58:38.065
First of all, it would be great
if the VA started sharing data,

00:58:38.065 --> 00:58:39.130
you know?

00:58:39.130 --> 00:58:40.930
I've talked to the
top people at the VA,

00:58:40.930 --> 00:58:43.060
and they're
philosophically aligned,

00:58:43.060 --> 00:58:44.289
but they don't share data.

00:58:44.289 --> 00:58:45.830
So that's an amazing
thing, you know?

00:58:45.830 --> 00:58:47.830
The federal government
pays for trials in the VA

00:58:47.830 --> 00:58:50.110
and the VA doesn't
share the data.

00:58:50.110 --> 00:58:53.680
They will say you can apply to
work with VA investigators who

00:58:53.680 --> 00:58:54.442
did this study.

00:58:54.442 --> 00:58:56.650
By the way, this is another
interesting thing, Brian.

00:58:56.650 --> 00:58:58.720
You know, a lot of the
things people are saying

00:58:58.720 --> 00:59:00.880
is, well, if you
want to do sharing,

00:59:00.880 --> 00:59:02.470
you must include
the investigators

00:59:02.470 --> 00:59:03.720
from the original study on it.

00:59:03.720 --> 00:59:06.430
When one of the
beliefs here is you

00:59:06.430 --> 00:59:10.294
need to separate from the
original investigators instead.

00:59:10.294 --> 00:59:11.710
And one of the
things that I would

00:59:11.710 --> 00:59:13.750
hear a lot from
established investigators

00:59:13.750 --> 00:59:15.850
were that no one
could possibly use

00:59:15.850 --> 00:59:17.230
my trial because
they could never

00:59:17.230 --> 00:59:18.779
understand the data like we do.

00:59:18.779 --> 00:59:21.070
And then I would say, but
what about the documentation?

00:59:21.070 --> 00:59:22.510
And the say, well, we
made a lot of decisions

00:59:22.510 --> 00:59:24.490
that we never documented
and that no one

00:59:24.490 --> 00:59:26.980
would be able to understand
all of the data definitions.

00:59:26.980 --> 00:59:28.560
And we basically
just said, bingo.

00:59:28.560 --> 00:59:30.310
You just said why
you've got to share data

00:59:30.310 --> 00:59:32.950
because you basically just
said you're unauditable.

00:59:32.950 --> 00:59:35.210
I'm not saying the VA,
but I'm just saying--

00:59:35.210 --> 00:59:36.460
no one at the VA told me that.

00:59:36.460 --> 00:59:38.704
I'm saying other
places, when I've

00:59:38.704 --> 00:59:40.120
heard that, people
have said that,

00:59:40.120 --> 00:59:42.730
and I said, that
without the self,

00:59:42.730 --> 00:59:45.460
sort of, insight that
what they've just said

00:59:45.460 --> 00:59:47.710
is actually something very
terrible about the quality

00:59:47.710 --> 00:59:49.210
of the science.

00:59:49.210 --> 00:59:52.330
But with regard to this-- yeah,
metadata has to accompany this.

00:59:52.330 --> 00:59:53.710
And the presumption is--

00:59:53.710 --> 00:59:55.330
what our belief
is going forward,

00:59:55.330 --> 00:59:57.820
if you knew your data
was going to be shared,

00:59:57.820 --> 00:59:59.620
then you would
take even more care

00:59:59.620 --> 01:00:02.020
to make sure the manuals
and the documentation

01:00:02.020 --> 01:00:03.910
and everything was very clear.

01:00:03.910 --> 01:00:05.345
When it's not going
to be shared--

01:00:05.345 --> 01:00:06.720
it was interesting
with J and J--

01:00:06.720 --> 01:00:07.540
they know this.

01:00:07.540 --> 01:00:10.120
It's not speaking out of school,
but a lot of their trials

01:00:10.120 --> 01:00:11.650
were in very poor
shape with regard

01:00:11.650 --> 01:00:14.410
to documentation and same
thing with Medtronics.

01:00:14.410 --> 01:00:18.100
A lot of the original time
was spent going back and sort

01:00:18.100 --> 01:00:20.770
of retrofitting like
the documentation,

01:00:20.770 --> 01:00:22.604
not for bias but
just definitions.

01:00:22.604 --> 01:00:24.770
They didn't even have the
stuff at their fingertips.

01:00:24.770 --> 01:00:26.978
And what we're saying is
good science should be like,

01:00:26.978 --> 01:00:29.477
immediately, you've got
all the data definitions.

01:00:29.477 --> 01:00:31.310
Anyone on the team can
get their hands on it

01:00:31.310 --> 01:00:32.800
and if you were
going to share, it's

01:00:32.800 --> 01:00:34.925
not a big deal to kind of
pull this stuff together.

01:00:34.925 --> 01:00:37.030
But for some reason,
we got into a habit,

01:00:37.030 --> 01:00:39.107
where maybe because it
was never being shared

01:00:39.107 --> 01:00:40.690
it was kind of like,
internally, there

01:00:40.690 --> 01:00:45.100
was a lot of communication, but
that people weren't keeping up

01:00:45.100 --> 01:00:48.310
the documentation in a way
that would make it easy.

01:00:48.310 --> 01:00:50.620
But yeah, on our site
and with J and J,

01:00:50.620 --> 01:00:52.644
we have to have all
that metadata in order

01:00:52.644 --> 01:00:53.560
to make it meaningful.

01:00:53.560 --> 01:00:55.159
You can't just share that data.

01:00:55.159 --> 01:00:56.950
And that takes work,
especially going back.

01:00:56.950 --> 01:00:58.690
What we want to see
is going forward,

01:00:58.690 --> 01:01:00.430
people are anticipating this.

01:01:00.430 --> 01:01:02.036
So that these
standards-- and this

01:01:02.036 --> 01:01:03.160
is another area to work on.

01:01:03.160 --> 01:01:05.260
What are the standards
of documentation

01:01:05.260 --> 01:01:07.840
that represent best
practice for other people

01:01:07.840 --> 01:01:10.930
to be able to use what
you've done or audit it?

01:01:10.930 --> 01:01:15.310
And those, as far as I can see,
are not codified completely.

01:01:15.310 --> 01:01:17.020
But yeah, it's very important.

01:01:17.020 --> 01:01:19.877
And I look to the day when
VA will share their data.

01:01:19.877 --> 01:01:22.210
Well, I think that right now
you can apply for that data

01:01:22.210 --> 01:01:27.030
but I think some of the projects
I see, I think they have--

01:01:27.030 --> 01:01:28.390
Like COURAGE trial.

01:01:28.390 --> 01:01:30.586
You can't get your hands
on the COURAGE trial.

01:01:30.586 --> 01:01:32.370
The investigators are
holding it so tight.

01:01:32.370 --> 01:01:33.750
And if I'm being filmed, hi.

01:01:33.750 --> 01:01:34.676
I know all of you.

01:01:34.676 --> 01:01:35.800
You're holding it so tight.

01:01:40.111 --> 01:01:41.245
It's late.

01:01:41.245 --> 01:01:43.760
I want to thank you all.

